# Navigation

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Navigation helps people reach rooms, dashboards and pages. Keep Home Assistant’s native sidebar, dashboard tabs and profile access, so that people have a reliable route to the same functions before and after applying Civica.

This page covers:

- Navigation
  - `<nav>`
  - Civica A-Z jump navigation pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A group of navigation links has:

- a name that says what it is for, such as “Rooms”
- a list of links, each with a text label and, where Home Assistant gives one, an icon
- one link marked as the current place
- a route back to the overview

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-nav"><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code></h3>

The browser’s element for a major block of navigation links, such as a site menu or a list of rooms. It holds flow content, takes only the global attributes, and is a landmark for assistive technology. Do not add `role="navigation"`. It has no styling of its own, so the links and list need styling.

- Use `<nav>` for the main blocks of links, not for every group of links.
- Give each `<nav>` on a page its own name with `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`, such as “Rooms”, without the word “navigation” in it.
- Put the links in a list.
- Mark the current link with `aria-current="page"` and a visible cue that is not only colour, such as bold text and a bar.

A dashboard author can write a `<nav>` only in HTML that Home Assistant lets them write, such as a custom card, and in documentation. A theme cannot add one. In a dashboard, use a navigation card, or a Markdown card with real links.

Home Assistant’s own sidebar and view tabs stay as they are. The Civica theme sets their colours: the navigation icon colour (`civica-navigation-icon-color`: `#484949` in light mode, `#CBD0D3` in dark mode), the active icon colour (`civica-navigation-icon-active-color`: `#19578D` in light mode, `#C0D7EB` in dark mode, in the default theme), the masthead icon colour (`#FFFFFF`) and the sidebar colours. The count on a sidebar item is described on [Badge](notification-badge.html).

<h3 id="child-civica-a-z-jump-navigation-pattern"><code>Civica A-Z jump navigation pattern</code></h3>

A row of letters that jumps to a long, single-subject list of guides, such as appliance guides. It is a Civica documentation pattern. Home Assistant has no A to Z control, and the theme adds none: it changes only the colour of the links. Build it from ordinary links in a Markdown card or a guide page.

[Browse household guides from A to Z](../patterns/a-to-z.html) owns the rules for letters, link size, headings, aliases and search.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A `<nav>` landmark lets people who use assistive technology jump straight to the links.
- Home Assistant’s sidebar and tabs are native, so keeping them keeps their built-in behaviour.
- A theme cannot add a `<nav>` to a dashboard.
- Several navigation bars that repeat the same destinations confuse people.
- An A to Z suits a long list on one subject. A short list is easier to use under headings.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

**Main navigation.** Use text labels with the icons Home Assistant already shows. Show the current place with a persistent indicator and `aria-current`, not only a colour change. Keep sidebar collapse, keyboard activation and narrow-screen navigation working. Do not turn a link that navigates into a button, or a button that acts into a link.

**Rooms and dashboards.** Use clear room names in a consistent order, and keep a visible route back to the overview. Do not stack navigation bars that repeat the same destinations. Use tabs for related views in one context, and keep the native tab keyboard behaviour. The theme colour selector is a form control, not a navigation tab.

**Brand placement.** The Civica mark takes the theme’s identity area, with enough space to stay legible. Do not cover the menu toggle or shrink targets to fit it. On a narrow screen, use the compact emblem only where the nearby text still names the service.

**Native panels.** Energy, History, Map and Settings, including Tools, keep their native routes. Do not add a duplicate Energy page that looks official but leaves out what the native panel offers.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: a landmark, with a list of links.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the current place is not shown by colour alone.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: links wrap at 320 pixels.
- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks: offer a [skip link](skip-link.html) before repeated navigation.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose and 2.4.5 Multiple Ways: names say where each link goes, and search or an A to Z gives another route.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): a fixed bar must not cover a focused link.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the A to Z letter links are 44 pixels square.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation and 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: the same order and names everywhere.

A few destinations, with familiar names, in the same order every time, keep the load low.

